Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces (@computing-source-os: 0, is #reference) { r_009CB72626232ED2E08E80BFC4
Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau. Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces. Arpaci-Dusseau Books, version 1.10, November 2023, as identified on the [authors' University of Wisconsin site](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/). The site organizes virtualization, concurrency and persistence, with additional security chapters credited to Peter Reiher.

Begin this common block with chapters 4 (Processes), 6 (Limited Direct Execution) and 18 (Paging), using chapter 13's Address Spaces and chapter 14's Memory API as progressive supporting reading. Work the appropriate introductory questions after the concepts are developed. The complete book, simulation exercises and implementation projects support later operating-system formation; their assignment is not a claim that those activities were performed or every chapter inspected.

On 31 October 2026 the author/version/contents page and selected text passages in the official [process chapter](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-intro.pdf), [controlled execution chapter](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-mechanisms.pdf) and [paging chapter](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-paging.pdf) were inspected for state, restricted operations and per-process translation. The PDFs identify version 1.10 and author copyright. Chapters 13–14, complete exercises, downloadable source and the larger book were not newly audited. Historical architecture examples are not current universal specifications.

Free author-hosted chapter access is distinct from permission to redistribute or adapt the whole work. Preserve the authors' copyright and component terms; no unrestricted reuse licence is inferred here. All PDFs, diagrams, source and course media remain external. The two-process service model, maps, programs and tests in this trail are original constructions, not copied textbook simulators or a reproduction of a full OS project.

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).

**Fifth computing-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the official author/version/contents page (version 1.10, November 2023), selected [chapter 39](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-intro.pdf) openings on files/directories and its file/directory synchronization distinction, and selected [chapter 42](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-journaling.pdf) passages on interrupted updates, persistence ordering, commit/recovery and section 42.4's copy-on-write approach. The PDFs retain author copyright/version notices. Read chapters 39 and 42 progressively, with device/filesystem chapters as supporting later preparation; their full examples, exercises and implementations were not reproduced or wholly audited. The new nine-word selector protocol, proofs, finite transition model and independent oracle are original constructions. They do not validate ext3, ZFS or a particular device/API. Free author access does not imply an unrestricted redistribution licence; source media remain external.


**Sixth computing-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the official author/version/contents page, retaining version 1.10, November 2023, and the author copyright/free-access distinction. Selected actual passages were inspected in [chapter 26](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-intro.pdf): opening thread state/shared-address-space discussion and the load/add/store trace around Figure 26.7; in [chapter 28](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-locks.pdf): basic locks, evaluation criteria, failed load/store synchronization, atomic test-and-set, fairness and performance; and in [chapter 30](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-cv.pdf): predicate state, atomic wait enrollment/release and reacquisition, missed notification and later while/recheck/spurious-wakeup discussion. Figure 26.7 on printed page 10 (PDF index 9) supplies a bounded abstract counter reconstruction: starting at 50, the selected interleaving ends at 51 instead of 52. No processor timing, address table, image or complete source program is reproduced. Read these chapters progressively and use their exercises as later work; complete chapters, projects, simulators and all implementations were not audited or executed. The finite actor, mutant-lock, predicate-wait and independent-oracle programs in this trail are original constructions. The wait model explicitly abstracts a synchronization boundary and is not a condition-variable implementation. Earlier source assignments and copyright/component qualifications remain; all source media stay external.


**Seventh computing-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the official author/contents page (version 1.10, November 2023) and actual chapter 48 [communication passages](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/dist-intro.pdf), sections 48.1–48.3 and selected 48.5 RPC discussion. Figures 48.4–48.5 supply the two bounded loss-order reconstructions in the original study. Read chapter 48 progressively; full exercises, socket code and implementations were not reproduced or fully audited. A timeout triggers policy without proving loss under the trail's declared asynchronous assumptions. Author copyright/free-access qualifications remain; source media stay external.

